The second land is still in the merge queue and the run is about to hit its time limit, so issue #101 stays open. The manager will land it after this run stops and post the verdict on the issue. The next run then posts the outcome comment and closes the issue, or fixes whatever fails.

**What's on the branch** (`looper/looper-101`, queued at 19:36Z):
- **`8960e10`, the fix:** the image now builds osv-scanner, trivy, gitleaks and syft with the patched libraries the issue asks for: `klauspost/compress` at v1.20.2 in osv-scanner, trivy and gitleaks, and `andybalholm/brotli` at v1.2.7 in gitleaks and syft. Trivy and osv-scanner now get their own build setup like gitleaks and syft already had. The image build checks that each patched version is the one actually built in. The README's Security checks table names each override and when it can be dropped. PROPOSALS.md has a new idea: have the image build run `govulncheck` on the scanners itself.
- **`1bd076d`, a test fix:** the first land at 19:09Z was refused. The machine that re-runs the tests now has the GitHub App settings (`LOOPER_REPORT_APP_ID` and four related variables) in its environment, so `tests/forge.test.sh` failed at `ready (github)`. I reproduced it and found that `tests/repo-looper.test.sh` and `tests/looper-dispatch.test.sh` fail the same way. Each of the three now clears those variables at its start; the tests that need them set them explicitly. While this goes unfixed on main, the same failure will likely refuse other issues' lands too.

**Verification:**
- **This run:** all 18 tests in the full test suite pass, both with the App variables set (as on the merge machine) and in an empty environment. The last lines include `forge: all scenarios pass`, `repo-looper: all scenarios pass`, `looper-dispatch: all scenarios pass` and `looper-verify: all passed`.
- **Not re-run here:** the image build, the tools' startup checks and version checks, and the `govulncheck` check that GO-2026-6865 and GO-2026-6888 are gone. The previous run's checkpoint says it did all of these with a clean result; the next run should rebuild the image and re-run them before closing.

**Other things to know:**
- At 18:43Z the looper posted on the issue that this run's job looked gone and removed its in-progress label, though the run was still working.
- One of my test runs printed this job's short-lived GitHub App token into a file in /tmp. I deleted the file right away; the token was never committed or posted anywhere.
